# A sample /STARTUP.BAT -- copy it to / and edit. It runs at power-on.
# If one you wrote wedges the machine, turn it off in F12 > Run STARTUP.BAT,
# or start the emulator with --no-startup.bat. Your own copy is never
# overwritten.
# Aliases live in RAM, so they are defined here every boot.

# the CP/M programs, each through a .SUB on A: that ends in EXIT,
# so quitting the program comes all the way back to this shell
ALIAS MBASIC CPM K-MBAS
ALIAS TURBO  CPM K-TURBO
ALIAS WS     CPM K-WS

# odds and ends.  These are the ones Doc actually kept on his own machine
# (2026-09-16), which is why they are the sample's: a short name for DIR, the
# two spellings a hand types without thinking, and a word for each folder you
# go back to.  A name may be upper or lower case; the shell does not care.
ALIAS LL DIR A
ALIAS d DIR
ALIAS ls dir
ALIAS dl dir -l
ALIAS tekplay !tekplay
ALIAS .. cd ..

# which game DOOM plays, from anywhere: the program lives in DOOM's own folder
ALIAS WADS /APPS/DOOM/WADCHOOSER
alias / cd /
alias less type
alias HOME cd /HOME
alias SYSTEM cd /SYSTEM
alias LANG cd /LANG
alias DOCUMENTS cd /DOCUMENTS

# BANNER reprints the power-on banner.  The machine has already drawn it by
# the time this file runs, so this is only worth uncommenting if your startup
# file printed over it -- a PALETTE LOAD or a COLOR clears the screen.
# banner

# the Linux beneath, in one word: a login there with its own tty, which is
# the same door as F12 > Host > Telnet into the host.  It asks for no
# password -- the telnet listens on the loopback and nowhere else, and `!`
# already opens a shell on that same Linux.  (`!` is a shell on the Tube;
# SSH goes to another computer.)
ALIAS HOST TELNET 127.0.0.1 23

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONSOLE COLOURS.  COLOR (or COLOUR) sets the foreground and, if you give
# a second value, the background.  Both are palette indices IN HEX, and it
# clears the screen as it takes effect -- which is why it belongs near the
# top of a startup file rather than the bottom.
#
#     COLOR fg
#     COLOR fg bg
#
# The palette is the C64's sixteen, in the C64's own order:
#
#     0  black          8  orange
#     1  white          9  brown
#     2  red            A  light red
#     3  cyan           B  dark grey
#     4  purple         C  grey
#     5  green          D  light green
#     6  blue           E  light blue
#     7  yellow         F  light grey
#
# The machine's own is yellow on blue, which is COLOR 7 6.  Some others
# worth trying:
#
#     COLOR D 0      light green on black   (a VDU)
#     COLOR 1 6      white on blue          (plainer than the default)
#     COLOR 8 0      amber on black         (a terminal)
#     COLOR 0 F      black on light grey    (paper)
#
# Uncomment one, or add your own:
#
# COLOR D 0
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE KEYBOARD LAYOUT IS NOT SET HERE.  It is F12 > Input > Keyboard
# layout (US, US-intl, Canada-FR, France, Germany, Spain, UK, Italy, or
# Host to leave it to the desktop or Linux beneath), kept in k4510.cfg;
# on the K4510's own Linux the consoles follow it.
#
# A PALETTE LOAD here makes a palette the machine's own at every boot:
#
# PALETTE LOAD GREEN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
